// @check
// @error argument 1: expected {tag: _}, got {r: f64}
// THE REFUSAL THAT SURVIVES THE RULING, in the INFERENCE HOLE'S OWN WORDS. An unbounded
// `<T>` is an open hole, so an argument that does not carry what the body read off the
// parameter is refused where the reader is standing — at the call, stating expected and
// got, naming no concept and prescribing no declaration.
//
// THE TWO SPELLINGS PRINT THE SAME SENTENCE. Replace `<T>(x: T)` with a bare `(x)` and
// the message is byte-identical at the same column: `holeArgDemandErr` renders the
// hole's grown shape, this renders the deferred rows, and they are the same demand
// (D1431). If they ever diverge, one of the two is wrong.
//
// The demand ACCUMULATES — a body reading two members refuses with both in the expected
// shape — which is why it is built from every row on the receiver rather than from the
// one that happened to fail.
function g<T>(x: T): string { x.tag() }

print(g({ r: 1.0 }))
